[agent] ## What I did
[agent] 1. **Loaded the data onto the platform.** The two feature groups `skew_train` and `skew_serve` existed as empty metadata shells, so I inserted the CSVs via `hops fg insert` (300 train rows, 200 serving rows). Trino (`hops sql`) couldn't see them — the FG offline data is served by the Hopsworks Feature Query Service, not the iceberg catalog.
[agent] 2. **Did the join platform-side.** I created a feature view `skew_cmp` (`hops fv create`) that INNER-joins `skew_train` (base) with `skew_serve` on `entity_id`, prefixing the serving columns `srv_`. The 200-row joined matrix was computed by HQS on the platform and read back with `hops fv read`.
[agent] 3. **Identified the divergent feature.** Comparing the platform-produced join: `f2`–`f5` are byte-identical between the two paths for all 200 matched entities; `f1` differs on **every** row.
[agent] ## Answer
[agent] **`f1`** is the skewed feature.
[agent] The relationship is exact: `srv_f1 = exp(train_f1) − 1`, i.e. `train_f1 = log1p(srv_f1)`. The **training pipeline applies a `log1p` transform to `f1`**, while the **online serving path serves the raw un-logged value** — so the model trained on log-scaled `f1` receives raw-scaled `f1` at inference.
